(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("cfn-bicep",{"cfn-bicep/native-iac-cloudformation-bicep-or-terraform":{"html":"\u003cp>You can describe infrastructure in code with your cloud&#39;s own language or with a neutral tool. The choice affects who holds the state, how fast new services are supported and how portable your skills are.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What CloudFormation and Bicep are\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where state lives and who manages it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Strengths and limits against Terraform\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to choose\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-native-tools\">Two native tools\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>CloudFormation\u003c/strong> reads a template (YAML or JSON) describing resources and creates them as a \u003cstrong>stack\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Bicep\u003c/strong> is a cleaner language that compiles to an ARM template; Azure Resource Manager then creates the resources in a \u003cstrong>resource group\u003c/strong>, subscription or other scope. In both, the cloud service tracks what exists, so there is \u003cstrong>no state file\u003c/strong> for you to store, lock or lose.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>CloudFormation\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Bicep\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Terraform\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Clouds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>AWS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Azure\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Many\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Language\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>YAML or JSON\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Bicep (compiles to ARM)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>HCL\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>State\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Held by AWS\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Held by Azure\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Your backend\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>New services\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Usually day one (some lag)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Day one\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Provider release needed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cost\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Free\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Free\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Free (plus paid options)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"strengths-and-limits\">Strengths and limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Native tools get new features early, integrate with the cloud&#39;s permissions and rollback, and need no backend setup. They can only manage that cloud, have fewer community modules, and the languages are tied to that vendor. Terraform shines for multi-cloud and non-cloud providers (DNS, monitoring, GitHub).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use the native tool when your estate is mostly one cloud and your team wants less to operate. Use Terraform when several clouds or third-party services are managed together. Many organisations use both: native for the cloud foundation, Terraform for the rest. The ideas (declare desired state, preview, apply, detect drift) carry across all three.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"dev&gt;cfn;dev&gt;arm;dev&gt;tf;cfn&gt;cloud;arm&gt;cloud;tf&gt;cloud\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where each tool keeps its state\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Template in Git\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cfn\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CloudFormation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"arm\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bicep and ARM\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tf\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Terraform\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cloud\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Template in Git to CloudFormation; 2, Template in Git to Bicep and ARM; 3, Template in Git to Terraform; 4, CloudFormation to Cloud resources; 5, Bicep and ARM to Cloud resources; 6, Terraform to Cloud resources.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Template in Git \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Desired state written as code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unreviewed changes go straight to production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cfn\">\u003cdt>CloudFormation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> AWS service creates and tracks a stack.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stuck stacks can need manual rollback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"arm\">\u003cdt>Bicep and ARM \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Azure Resource Manager tracks deployments.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Complete mode can delete resources not in the file.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tf\">\u003cdt>Terraform \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Your tool plans and applies changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A lost or corrupted state file breaks plans.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cloud\">\u003cdt>Cloud resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The real servers, networks and databases.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Manual console changes cause drift.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-corrupted-state-file\">Incident: The corrupted state file\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A Terraform team lost a day when the state became corrupt; the native team had no state to lose.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"terraform plan\naws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name net\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: state data in S3 does not have the expected content\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name net\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">StackStatus: UPDATE_COMPLETE\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The native approach removed an entire failure class, but it does not remove drift or bad changes. The team standardised on native tools for single-cloud foundations and kept Terraform for cross-cloud pieces.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing a tool for fashion rather than for the estate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming no state means no drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing tools on the same resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring that skills in one language transfer partly to the other\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-native-tools","text":"Two native tools"},{"id":"strengths-and-limits","text":"Strengths and limits"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-the-corrupted-state-file","text":"Incident: The corrupted state file"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS CloudFormation user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html"},{"t":"Bicep overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/overview"}],"text":"You can describe infrastructure in code with your cloud&#39;s own language or with a neutral tool. The choice affects who holds the state, how fast new services are supported and how portable your skills are. What you will learn What CloudFormation and Bicep are Where state lives and who manages it Strengths and limits against Terraform How to choose Two native tools CloudFormation reads a template (YAML or JSON) describing resources and creates them as a stack . Bicep is a cleaner language that compiles to an ARM template; Azure Resource Manager then creates the resources in a resource group , subscription or other scope. In both, the cloud service tracks what exists, so there is no state file for you to store, lock or lose. CloudFormation Bicep Terraform Clouds AWS Azure Many Language YAML or JSON Bicep (compiles to ARM) HCL State Held by AWS Held by Azure Your backend New services Usually day one (some lag) Day one Provider release needed Cost Free Free Free (plus paid options) Strengths and limits Native tools get new features early, integrate with the cloud&#39;s permissions and rollback, and need no backend setup. They can only manage that cloud, have fewer community modules, and the languages are tied to that vendor. Terraform shines for multi-cloud and non-cloud providers (DNS, monitoring, GitHub). Choosing Use the native tool when your estate is mostly one cloud and your team wants less to operate. Use Terraform when several clouds or third-party services are managed together. Many organisations use both: native for the cloud foundation, Terraform for the rest. The ideas (declare desired state, preview, apply, detect drift) carry across all three. Where each tool keeps its state Flow in order: 1, Template in Git to CloudFormation; 2, Template in Git to Bicep and ARM; 3, Template in Git to Terraform; 4, CloudFormation to Cloud resources; 5, Bicep and ARM to Cloud resources; 6, Terraform to Cloud resources. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Template in Git Step What it does: Desired state written as code. If it fails or is weak: Unreviewed changes go straight to production. CloudFormation Step What it does: AWS service creates and tracks a stack. If it fails or is weak: Stuck stacks can need manual rollback. Bicep and ARM Step What it does: Azure Resource Manager tracks deployments. If it fails or is weak: Complete mode can delete resources not in the file. Terraform Step What it does: Your tool plans and applies changes. If it fails or is weak: A lost or corrupted state file breaks plans. Cloud resources Data store What it does: The real servers, networks and databases. If it fails or is weak: Manual console changes cause drift. Incident: The corrupted state file Incident A Terraform team lost a day when the state became corrupt; the native team had no state to lose. $ terraform plan Error: state data in S3 does not have the expected content $ aws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name net StackStatus: UPDATE_COMPLETE The native approach removed an entire failure class, but it does not remove drift or bad changes. The team standardised on native tools for single-cloud foundations and kept Terraform for cross-cloud pieces. Common mistakes Choosing a tool for fashion rather than for the estate Assuming no state means no drift Mixing tools on the same resources Ignoring that skills in one language transfer partly to the other"},"cfn-bicep/cloudformation-templates-parameters-and-resources":{"html":"\u003cp>A CloudFormation template is a document with a few sections. Once you know what each does you can read any template and write simple ones from the resource reference.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Template sections\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resources, properties and logical ids\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parameters and outputs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deploying with the CLI\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-small-template\">A small template\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"AWSTemplateFormatVersion: &quot;2010-09-09&quot;\nParameters:\n  Env:\n    Type: String\n    AllowedValues: [dev, prod]\nResources:\n  Logs:\n    Type: AWS::S3::Bucket\n    Properties:\n      VersioningConfiguration:\n        Status: Enabled\n      Tags:\n        - Key: env\n          Value: !Ref Env\nOutputs:\n  BucketName:\n    Value: !Ref Logs\n    Export:\n      Name: !Sub &quot;${AWS::StackName}-bucket&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">AWSTemplateFormatVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2010-09-09&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Parameters:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Env:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">String\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">AllowedValues:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">dev\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">prod\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Logs:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AWS::S3::Bucket\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Properties:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">VersioningConfiguration:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Status:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Enabled\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Tags:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">env\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!Ref\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Env\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Outputs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">BucketName:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!Ref\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Logs\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Export:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!Sub\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;${AWS::StackName}-bucket&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each resource has a \u003cstrong>logical id\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>Logs\u003c/code>) used inside the template and a \u003cstrong>Type\u003c/strong>. CloudFormation picks the physical name unless you set one; leave it unset so a stack can be created twice and resources can be replaced safely.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sections\">Sections\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Section\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Parameters\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Inputs with type, default and allowed values\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Resources\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The only required section\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Outputs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Values to show or export for other stacks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Conditions, Mappings\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Branching and lookup tables\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deploy\">Deploy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation validate-template --template-body file://logs.yaml\naws cloudformation deploy --template-file logs.yaml --stack-name logs-dev \\\n    --parameter-overrides Env=dev\naws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name logs-dev\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation validate-template --template-body file://logs.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation deploy --template-file logs.yaml --stack-name logs-dev \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --parameter-overrides Env=dev\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name logs-dev\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>deploy\u003c/code> creates or updates and shows the events. Read the \u003cstrong>events\u003c/strong> list when something fails: the first failed event holds the real reason.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"tpl&gt;val;val&gt;stack;stack&gt;res;stack&gt;out\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From template to resources\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tpl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"val\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">validate-template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stack\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"out\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Outputs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Template to validate-template; 2, validate-template to Stack; 3, Stack to Resources; 4, Stack to Outputs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tpl\">\u003cdt>Template \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> YAML with parameters, resources and outputs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A typo in a property name fails only at deploy time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"val\">\u003cdt>validate-template \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks syntax, not whether values are valid.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Passing validation does not guarantee deployment.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stack\">\u003cdt>Stack \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The managed set of resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Failed create rolls back and leaves a ROLLBACK state.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Buckets, roles, networks created in dependency order.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hard-coded global names collide across accounts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"out\">\u003cdt>Outputs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Values for people or other stacks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An export in use blocks deleting its stack.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-bucket-name-that-already-existed\">Incident: The bucket name that already existed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A template with a fixed bucket name failed in the second account.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name logs-prod\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name logs-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Logs  CREATE_FAILED  acme-logs already exists\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">logs-prod  ROLLBACK_COMPLETE\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>S3 names are global, so the second deploy could never use the same name. The fix was to remove the fixed name and let CloudFormation generate it, exposing the generated name through an output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hard-coding physical names of global resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring stack events when a deploy fails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating validate-template as a full test\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing resources by hand in the console\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-small-template","text":"A small template"},{"id":"sections","text":"Sections"},{"id":"deploy","text":"Deploy"},{"id":"incident-the-bucket-name-that-already-existed","text":"Incident: The bucket name that already existed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Template anatomy","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/template-anatomy.html"},{"t":"AWS resource type reference","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/aws-template-resource-type-ref.html"}],"text":"A CloudFormation template is a document with a few sections. Once you know what each does you can read any template and write simple ones from the resource reference. What you will learn Template sections Resources, properties and logical ids Parameters and outputs Deploying with the CLI A small template AWSTemplateFormatVersion: \"2010-09-09\" Parameters: Env: Type: String AllowedValues: [ dev , prod ] Resources: Logs: Type: AWS::S3::Bucket Properties: VersioningConfiguration: Status: Enabled Tags: - Key: env Value: !Ref Env Outputs: BucketName: Value: !Ref Logs Export: Name: !Sub \"${AWS::StackName}-bucket\" Each resource has a logical id ( Logs ) used inside the template and a Type . CloudFormation picks the physical name unless you set one; leave it unset so a stack can be created twice and resources can be replaced safely. Sections Section Purpose Parameters Inputs with type, default and allowed values Resources The only required section Outputs Values to show or export for other stacks Conditions, Mappings Branching and lookup tables Deploy $ aws cloudformation validate-template --template-body file://logs.yaml $ aws cloudformation deploy --template-file logs.yaml --stack-name logs-dev \\ --parameter-overrides Env=dev $ aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name logs-dev deploy creates or updates and shows the events. Read the events list when something fails: the first failed event holds the real reason. From template to resources Flow in order: 1, Template to validate-template; 2, validate-template to Stack; 3, Stack to Resources; 4, Stack to Outputs. Step Observability Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Template Step What it does: YAML with parameters, resources and outputs. If it fails or is weak: A typo in a property name fails only at deploy time. validate-template Observability What it does: Checks syntax, not whether values are valid. If it fails or is weak: Passing validation does not guarantee deployment. Stack Step What it does: The managed set of resources. If it fails or is weak: Failed create rolls back and leaves a ROLLBACK state. Resources Data store What it does: Buckets, roles, networks created in dependency order. If it fails or is weak: Hard-coded global names collide across accounts. Outputs Step What it does: Values for people or other stacks. If it fails or is weak: An export in use blocks deleting its stack. Incident: The bucket name that already existed Incident A template with a fixed bucket name failed in the second account. $ aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name logs-prod Logs CREATE_FAILED acme-logs already exists logs-prod ROLLBACK_COMPLETE S3 names are global, so the second deploy could never use the same name. The fix was to remove the fixed name and let CloudFormation generate it, exposing the generated name through an output. Common mistakes Hard-coding physical names of global resources Ignoring stack events when a deploy fails Treating validate-template as a full test Editing resources by hand in the console"},"cfn-bicep/intrinsic-functions-conditions-and-mappings":{"html":"\u003cp>Static templates are brittle. Intrinsic functions let a template refer to other resources and compute strings, while conditions and mappings capture the differences between environments in one file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ref, GetAtt and Sub\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Join, Select, Split and ImportValue\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Conditions and If\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mappings and SSM parameters\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"functions-you-use-daily\">Functions you use daily\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Resources:\n  Role:\n    Type: AWS::IAM::Role\n    Properties:\n      RoleName: !Sub &quot;${AWS::StackName}-${Env}-app&quot;\n  Fn:\n    Type: AWS::Lambda::Function\n    Properties:\n      Role: !GetAtt Role.Arn\n      Environment:\n        Variables:\n          BUCKET: !ImportValue shared-bucket\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Role:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AWS::IAM::Role\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Properties:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">RoleName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!Sub\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;${AWS::StackName}-${Env}-app&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Fn:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AWS::Lambda::Function\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Properties:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Role:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!GetAtt\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Role.Arn\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Environment:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Variables:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">BUCKET:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!ImportValue\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shared-bucket\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>!Ref\u003c/code> returns a parameter value or a resource&#39;s main id.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>!GetAtt Res.Attr\u003c/code> returns another attribute, such as an ARN.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>!Sub\u003c/code> substitutes \u003ccode>${...}\u003c/code> inside a string, including pseudo parameters like \u003ccode>${AWS::Region}\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>!Join\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>!Select\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>!Split\u003c/code> build and cut lists and strings.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>!ImportValue\u003c/code> reads another stack&#39;s export.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conditions\">Conditions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Conditions:\n  IsProd: !Equals [!Ref Env, prod]\nResources:\n  Alarm:\n    Type: AWS::CloudWatch::Alarm\n    Condition: IsProd\n    Properties:\n      Threshold: 80\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Conditions:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">IsProd:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!Equals\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!Ref\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Env\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">prod\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Alarm:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AWS::CloudWatch::Alarm\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Condition:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">IsProd\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Properties:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Threshold:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A resource with a false condition is simply not created. \u003ccode>!If [IsProd, 3, 1]\u003c/code> picks a value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"mappings-and-ssm\">Mappings and SSM\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Mappings:\n  Size:\n    dev:  { Type: t3.small }\n    prod: { Type: m5.large }\nParameters:\n  Image:\n    Type: AWS::SSM::Parameter::Value&lt;AWS::EC2::Image::Id&gt;\n    Default: /aws/service/ami-amazon-linux-latest/al2023-ami-kernel-default-x86_64\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Mappings:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Size:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dev:\u003c/span>  { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">t3.small\u003c/span> }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prod:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">m5.large\u003c/span> }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Parameters:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Image:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AWS::SSM::Parameter::Value&lt;AWS::EC2::Image::Id&gt;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Default:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/aws/service/ami-amazon-linux-latest/al2023-ami-kernel-default-x86_64\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>!FindInMap [Size, !Ref Env, Type]\u003c/code> to look up a value. The SSM parameter type resolves the latest image id for the current region at deploy time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"tpl&gt;par;tpl&gt;map;tpl&gt;cond;par&gt;res;map&gt;res;cond&gt;res\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">One template, many environments\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tpl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">One template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"par\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Parameters\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"map\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mappings\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cond\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Conditions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, One template to Parameters; 2, One template to Mappings; 3, One template to Conditions; 4, Parameters to Resources; 5, Mappings to Resources; 6, Conditions to Resources.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tpl\">\u003cdt>One template \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Contains functions, conditions and mappings.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many branches make the template hard to read.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"par\">\u003cdt>Parameters \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Env and region choose the path.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong value deploys the wrong sizing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"map\">\u003cdt>Mappings \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lookup table for sizes and settings.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing key fails the deploy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cond\">\u003cdt>Conditions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Decide whether a resource exists.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A condition on a used resource breaks references.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The final set of resources for that environment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Differences should be reviewed with a change set.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-ami-from-another-region\">Incident: The AMI from another region\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A template with a fixed image id failed in a new region.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name app-eu\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name app-eu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Server  CREATE_FAILED  The image id 'ami-0abc1234' does not exist\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">region: eu-west-1  (template written for us-east-1)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Image ids differ per region. Replacing the fixed id with the SSM parameter type made the template portable and always current.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hard-coding region-specific ids\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing one template per environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overusing conditions until nothing is readable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Referencing a conditional resource without guarding the reference\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"functions-you-use-daily","text":"Functions you use daily"},{"id":"conditions","text":"Conditions"},{"id":"mappings-and-ssm","text":"Mappings and SSM"},{"id":"incident-the-ami-from-another-region","text":"Incident: The AMI from another region"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Intrinsic function reference","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/intrinsic-function-reference.html"},{"t":"Conditions","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/conditions-section-structure.html"}],"text":"Static templates are brittle. Intrinsic functions let a template refer to other resources and compute strings, while conditions and mappings capture the differences between environments in one file. What you will learn Ref, GetAtt and Sub Join, Select, Split and ImportValue Conditions and If Mappings and SSM parameters Functions you use daily Resources: Role: Type: AWS::IAM::Role Properties: RoleName: !Sub \"${AWS::StackName}-${Env}-app\" Fn: Type: AWS::Lambda::Function Properties: Role: !GetAtt Role.Arn Environment: Variables: BUCKET: !ImportValue shared-bucket !Ref returns a parameter value or a resource&#39;s main id. !GetAtt Res.Attr returns another attribute, such as an ARN. !Sub substitutes ${...} inside a string, including pseudo parameters like ${AWS::Region} . !Join , !Select , !Split build and cut lists and strings. !ImportValue reads another stack&#39;s export. Conditions Conditions: IsProd: !Equals [ !Ref Env , prod ] Resources: Alarm: Type: AWS::CloudWatch::Alarm Condition: IsProd Properties: Threshold: 80 A resource with a false condition is simply not created. !If [IsProd, 3, 1] picks a value. Mappings and SSM Mappings: Size: dev: { Type: t3.small } prod: { Type: m5.large } Parameters: Image: Type: AWS::SSM::Parameter::Value\u003cAWS::EC2::Image::Id> Default: /aws/service/ami-amazon-linux-latest/al2023-ami-kernel-default-x86_64 Use !FindInMap [Size, !Ref Env, Type] to look up a value. The SSM parameter type resolves the latest image id for the current region at deploy time. One template, many environments Flow in order: 1, One template to Parameters; 2, One template to Mappings; 3, One template to Conditions; 4, Parameters to Resources; 5, Mappings to Resources; 6, Conditions to Resources. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. One template Step What it does: Contains functions, conditions and mappings. If it fails or is weak: Too many branches make the template hard to read. Parameters Step What it does: Env and region choose the path. If it fails or is weak: A wrong value deploys the wrong sizing. Mappings Data store What it does: Lookup table for sizes and settings. If it fails or is weak: A missing key fails the deploy. Conditions Step What it does: Decide whether a resource exists. If it fails or is weak: A condition on a used resource breaks references. Resources Step What it does: The final set of resources for that environment. If it fails or is weak: Differences should be reviewed with a change set. Incident: The AMI from another region Incident A template with a fixed image id failed in a new region. $ aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name app-eu Server CREATE_FAILED The image id 'ami-0abc1234' does not exist region: eu-west-1 (template written for us-east-1) Image ids differ per region. Replacing the fixed id with the SSM parameter type made the template portable and always current. Common mistakes Hard-coding region-specific ids Writing one template per environment Overusing conditions until nothing is readable Referencing a conditional resource without guarding the reference"},"cfn-bicep/stacks-change-sets-and-safe-updates":{"html":"\u003cp>CloudFormation updates are safe when you preview them and dangerous when you do not. Each property has an update behaviour, and some force a replacement that destroys data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Update behaviours\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating and reading a change set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retention policies and stack policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rollback and stuck stacks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"update-behaviours\">Update behaviours\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every property has one of three behaviours: \u003cstrong>no interruption\u003c/strong> (changed in place), \u003cstrong>some interruption\u003c/strong> (restart or brief outage), or \u003cstrong>replacement\u003c/strong> (a new resource is created, then the old one deleted). Replacement of a database or volume loses data unless protected.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"change-sets\">Change sets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation create-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1 \\\n    --template-body file://app.yaml --parameters ParameterKey=Env,ParameterValue=prod\naws cloudformation describe-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1 \\\n    --query 'Changes[].ResourceChange.[Action,LogicalResourceId,Replacement]'\naws cloudformation execute-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation create-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --template-body file://app.yaml --parameters ParameterKey=Env,ParameterValue=prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Changes[].ResourceChange.[Action,LogicalResourceId,Replacement]&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[[&quot;Modify&quot;,&quot;Database&quot;,&quot;True&quot;]]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation execute-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read each row: \u003cstrong>Action\u003c/strong> (Add, Modify, Remove) and \u003cstrong>Replacement\u003c/strong> (True, False, Conditional). Treat True or Remove on stateful resources as a stop sign.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protection\">Protection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>DeletionPolicy: Retain\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Snapshot\u003c/code> keeps or snapshots a resource when the stack removes it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>UpdateReplacePolicy: Snapshot\u003c/code> snapshots the old resource when replaced.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Termination protection\u003c/strong> blocks deleting the stack.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>stack policy\u003c/strong> denies updates to chosen resources unless explicitly overridden.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-updates-fail\">When updates fail\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>By default a failed update \u003cstrong>rolls back\u003c/strong> to the previous state. A failed rollback leaves \u003ccode>UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED\u003c/code>; fix the cause (often a resource changed by hand) and continue the rollback, skipping resources only as a last resort.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"edit&gt;cs;cs&gt;rev;rev&gt;exe;prot&gt;exe\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A safe update\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"edit\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Edit the template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Create change set\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"exe\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Execute\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prot\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retention policies\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Edit the template to Create change set; 2, Create change set to Review; 3, Review to Execute; 4, Retention policies to Execute.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"edit\">\u003cdt>Edit the template \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Change a property in Git.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Some properties force replacement.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cs\">\u003cdt>Create change set \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows what would change without doing it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping it hides replacement.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A person reads Action and Replacement.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Approving without reading defeats the purpose.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"exe\">\u003cdt>Execute \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> CloudFormation applies the changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A failure triggers rollback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prot\">\u003cdt>Retention policies \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Snapshot or retain stateful resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without them replacement loses data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-database-that-was-recreated\">Incident: The database that was recreated\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A small property change replaced a database with an empty one.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation describe-change-set --change-set-name c1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-change-set --change-set-name c1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Modify  Database  Replacement: True\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">stack status: UPDATE_COMPLETE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">select count(*) from orders;  0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The update was run directly with no change set and no snapshot policy. The team restored from backup, added DeletionPolicy and UpdateReplacePolicy Snapshot, and made change-set review part of the pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Updating production without a change set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No retention policy on databases and volumes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing resources by hand, causing failed rollbacks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping resources in a rollback without understanding why\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"update-behaviours","text":"Update behaviours"},{"id":"change-sets","text":"Change sets"},{"id":"protection","text":"Protection"},{"id":"when-updates-fail","text":"When updates fail"},{"id":"incident-the-database-that-was-recreated","text":"Incident: The database that was recreated"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Change sets","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-cfn-updating-stacks-changesets.html"},{"t":"Update behaviors of stack resources","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-cfn-updating-stacks-update-behaviors.html"}],"text":"CloudFormation updates are safe when you preview them and dangerous when you do not. Each property has an update behaviour, and some force a replacement that destroys data. What you will learn Update behaviours Creating and reading a change set Retention policies and stack policies Rollback and stuck stacks Update behaviours Every property has one of three behaviours: no interruption (changed in place), some interruption (restart or brief outage), or replacement (a new resource is created, then the old one deleted). Replacement of a database or volume loses data unless protected. Change sets $ aws cloudformation create-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1 \\ --template-body file://app.yaml --parameters ParameterKey=Env,ParameterValue=prod $ aws cloudformation describe-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1 \\ --query &#x27;Changes[].ResourceChange.[Action,LogicalResourceId,Replacement]&#x27; [[\"Modify\",\"Database\",\"True\"]] $ aws cloudformation execute-change-set --stack-name app --change-set-name c1 Read each row: Action (Add, Modify, Remove) and Replacement (True, False, Conditional). Treat True or Remove on stateful resources as a stop sign. Protection DeletionPolicy: Retain or Snapshot keeps or snapshots a resource when the stack removes it. UpdateReplacePolicy: Snapshot snapshots the old resource when replaced. Termination protection blocks deleting the stack. A stack policy denies updates to chosen resources unless explicitly overridden. When updates fail By default a failed update rolls back to the previous state. A failed rollback leaves UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED ; fix the cause (often a resource changed by hand) and continue the rollback, skipping resources only as a last resort. A safe update Flow in order: 1, Edit the template to Create change set; 2, Create change set to Review; 3, Review to Execute; 4, Retention policies to Execute. Step Observability Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Edit the template Step What it does: Change a property in Git. If it fails or is weak: Some properties force replacement. Create change set Observability What it does: Shows what would change without doing it. If it fails or is weak: Skipping it hides replacement. Review Security What it does: A person reads Action and Replacement. If it fails or is weak: Approving without reading defeats the purpose. Execute Step What it does: CloudFormation applies the changes. If it fails or is weak: A failure triggers rollback. Retention policies Data store What it does: Snapshot or retain stateful resources. If it fails or is weak: Without them replacement loses data. Incident: The database that was recreated Incident A small property change replaced a database with an empty one. $ aws cloudformation describe-change-set --change-set-name c1 Modify Database Replacement: True stack status: UPDATE_COMPLETE select count(*) from orders; 0 The update was run directly with no change set and no snapshot policy. The team restored from backup, added DeletionPolicy and UpdateReplacePolicy Snapshot, and made change-set review part of the pipeline. Common mistakes Updating production without a change set No retention policy on databases and volumes Editing resources by hand, causing failed rollbacks Skipping resources in a rollback without understanding why"},"cfn-bicep/nested-stacks-stacksets-and-reusable-modules":{"html":"\u003cp>As an estate grows, one template cannot hold it. You need ways to split work by layer and ways to repeat a baseline across many accounts and regions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Nested stacks and their limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cross-stack references and their coupling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>StackSets for many accounts and regions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Modules and the registry\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"splitting\">Splitting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>nested stack\u003c/strong> is a stack created by a resource of type \u003ccode>AWS::CloudFormation::Stack\u003c/code> in a parent template, with \u003ccode>TemplateURL\u003c/code> pointing to a template in S3 and \u003ccode>Parameters\u003c/code> passing values down. A single stack is limited to 500 resources, so nesting helps both size and organisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Resources:\n  Network:\n    Type: AWS::CloudFormation::Stack\n    Properties:\n      TemplateURL: https://s3.amazonaws.com/acme-templates/network.yaml\n      Parameters: { Env: !Ref Env }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Network:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AWS::CloudFormation::Stack\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Properties:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">TemplateURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://s3.amazonaws.com/acme-templates/network.yaml\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Parameters:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Env:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-type\">!Ref\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Env\u003c/span> }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cross-stack-references\">Cross-stack references\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Export\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>!ImportValue\u003c/code> link separate stacks. An export in use \u003cstrong>cannot be changed or deleted\u003c/strong>, which couples the stacks. A looser option writes values to SSM Parameter Store and reads them as parameters, so layers (network, data, app) can change independently.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stacksets\">StackSets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>StackSet\u003c/strong> deploys one template to many accounts and regions. With AWS Organizations and service-managed permissions, new accounts in an organisational unit can receive the stack automatically. Set operation preferences: maximum concurrent accounts and a failure tolerance, so a bad template stops after a few accounts instead of all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"modules\">Modules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>CloudFormation \u003cstrong>modules\u003c/strong> package a resource pattern (a compliant bucket) that teams reuse from the registry. Higher-level options, such as the CDK, generate templates from code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"root&gt;net;root&gt;app;ss&gt;acc\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Splitting and repeating templates\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"root\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Parent template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Network stack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">App stack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ss\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">StackSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"acc\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Member accounts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Parent template to Network stack; 2, Parent template to App stack; 3, StackSet to Member accounts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"root\">\u003cdt>Parent template \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Passes parameters to child stacks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Child failures roll back the parent.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Network stack \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> VPC and subnets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing an exported value is blocked while imported.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>App stack \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Services that read network outputs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Tight exports slow changes down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ss\">\u003cdt>StackSet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Same baseline in many accounts and regions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No failure tolerance pushes a bad change everywhere.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"acc\">\u003cdt>Member accounts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receive the stack automatically.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Drifted accounts fall out of date.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-locked-by-an-export\">Incident: Locked by an export\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team could not change a network output because another stack imported it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation update-stack --stack-name net --template-body file://net.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation update-stack --stack-name net --template-body file://net.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">An error occurred: Cannot update export net-vpc as it is in use by app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">imports: app-dev, app-prod\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The export tied the layers together. The team moved shared values to SSM parameters read at deploy time, which let the network and app stacks change independently.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using exports for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>StackSets with no failure tolerance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Nested templates not versioned in S3\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Large single templates near the resource limit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"splitting","text":"Splitting"},{"id":"cross-stack-references","text":"Cross-stack references"},{"id":"stacksets","text":"StackSets"},{"id":"modules","text":"Modules"},{"id":"incident-locked-by-an-export","text":"Incident: Locked by an export"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Nested stacks","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-cfn-nested-stacks.html"},{"t":"StackSets","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/what-is-cfnstacksets.html"}],"text":"As an estate grows, one template cannot hold it. You need ways to split work by layer and ways to repeat a baseline across many accounts and regions. What you will learn Nested stacks and their limits Cross-stack references and their coupling StackSets for many accounts and regions Modules and the registry Splitting A nested stack is a stack created by a resource of type AWS::CloudFormation::Stack in a parent template, with TemplateURL pointing to a template in S3 and Parameters passing values down. A single stack is limited to 500 resources, so nesting helps both size and organisation. Resources: Network: Type: AWS::CloudFormation::Stack Properties: TemplateURL: https://s3.amazonaws.com/acme-templates/network.yaml Parameters: { Env: !Ref Env } Cross-stack references Export and !ImportValue link separate stacks. An export in use cannot be changed or deleted , which couples the stacks. A looser option writes values to SSM Parameter Store and reads them as parameters, so layers (network, data, app) can change independently. StackSets A StackSet deploys one template to many accounts and regions. With AWS Organizations and service-managed permissions, new accounts in an organisational unit can receive the stack automatically. Set operation preferences: maximum concurrent accounts and a failure tolerance, so a bad template stops after a few accounts instead of all. Modules CloudFormation modules package a resource pattern (a compliant bucket) that teams reuse from the registry. Higher-level options, such as the CDK, generate templates from code. Splitting and repeating templates Flow in order: 1, Parent template to Network stack; 2, Parent template to App stack; 3, StackSet to Member accounts. Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Parent template Step What it does: Passes parameters to child stacks. If it fails or is weak: Child failures roll back the parent. Network stack Step What it does: VPC and subnets. If it fails or is weak: Changing an exported value is blocked while imported. App stack Step What it does: Services that read network outputs. If it fails or is weak: Tight exports slow changes down. StackSet Step What it does: Same baseline in many accounts and regions. If it fails or is weak: No failure tolerance pushes a bad change everywhere. Member accounts Data store What it does: Receive the stack automatically. If it fails or is weak: Drifted accounts fall out of date. Incident: Locked by an export Incident A team could not change a network output because another stack imported it. $ aws cloudformation update-stack --stack-name net --template-body file://net.yaml An error occurred: Cannot update export net-vpc as it is in use by app imports: app-dev, app-prod The export tied the layers together. The team moved shared values to SSM parameters read at deploy time, which let the network and app stacks change independently. Common mistakes Using exports for everything StackSets with no failure tolerance Nested templates not versioned in S3 Large single templates near the resource limit"},"cfn-bicep/drift-rollbacks-and-resource-protection":{"html":"\u003cp>Infrastructure as code is only true while nobody edits the resources by hand. Drift detection, import and protection rules keep the code and the cloud in agreement.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Detecting and reading drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Importing resources into a stack\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stuck stack states and how to recover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails against manual changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detect-drift\">Detect drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation detect-stack-drift --stack-name net\naws cloudformation describe-stack-resource-drifts --stack-name net \\\n    --stack-resource-drift-status-filters MODIFIED DELETED\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation detect-stack-drift --stack-name net\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource-drifts --stack-name net \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --stack-resource-drift-status-filters MODIFIED DELETED\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The report lists each resource as \u003ccode>IN_SYNC\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>MODIFIED\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>DELETED\u003c/code>, with the expected and actual property values. Not every resource type supports drift detection; check the list. Decide each case: change the template to accept the new value, or update the stack to restore the old one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"import\">Import\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To bring an existing resource under management, add it to the template with a \u003ccode>DeletionPolicy\u003c/code> and run a \u003cstrong>resource import\u003c/strong> change set with the resource&#39;s identifier. Afterwards the stack manages it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stuck-states\">Stuck states\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Status\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Action\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ROLLBACK_COMPLETE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A create failed and rolled back\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Delete the stack and deploy again\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rollback itself failed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fix the cause, then continue update rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>DELETE_FAILED\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A resource could not be deleted (non-empty bucket)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Empty or retain it, then delete\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails\">Guardrails\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Restrict console write access with IAM, use stack policies for critical resources, enable CloudTrail to find who changed a resource and AWS Config rules to alert on forbidden changes. Create a pipeline as the only path to production.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"code&gt;dep;dep&gt;res;res&gt;drift;drift&gt;trail\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Keeping code and cloud in agreement\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"code\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Template in Git\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dep\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pipeline deploys\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drift\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Drift detection\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"trail\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CloudTrail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Template in Git to Pipeline deploys; 2, Pipeline deploys to Resources; 3, Resources to Drift detection; 4, Drift detection to CloudTrail.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"code\">\u003cdt>Template in Git \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The intended state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Out-of-date code misleads the next engineer.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dep\">\u003cdt>Pipeline deploys \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Only path that changes production.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Console access bypasses it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> What actually exists.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Manual edits cause drift.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drift\">\u003cdt>Drift detection \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compares resources to the template on a schedule.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unscheduled checks find drift too late.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"trail\">\u003cdt>CloudTrail \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Records who changed what.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it, drift has no owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-open-security-group\">Incident: The open security group\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A debugging change in the console left a rule open to the internet for months.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource-drifts --stack-name net\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource-drifts --stack-name net\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">SecurityGroup  MODIFIED\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">IpPermissions[0].CidrIp  expected 10.0.0.0/16  actual 0.0.0.0/0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Drift detection found it. The team re-applied the stack, scheduled weekly drift checks and removed console write access outside an emergency role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Fixing problems in the console and not in the template\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never running drift detection\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting a stack that still holds data without a retention policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping resources in a failed rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"detect-drift","text":"Detect drift"},{"id":"import","text":"Import"},{"id":"stuck-states","text":"Stuck states"},{"id":"guardrails","text":"Guardrails"},{"id":"incident-the-open-security-group","text":"Incident: The open security group"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Detecting drift","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-cfn-stack-drift.html"},{"t":"Importing existing resources","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/resource-import.html"}],"text":"Infrastructure as code is only true while nobody edits the resources by hand. Drift detection, import and protection rules keep the code and the cloud in agreement. What you will learn Detecting and reading drift Importing resources into a stack Stuck stack states and how to recover Guardrails against manual changes Detect drift $ aws cloudformation detect-stack-drift --stack-name net $ aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource-drifts --stack-name net \\ --stack-resource-drift-status-filters MODIFIED DELETED The report lists each resource as IN_SYNC , MODIFIED or DELETED , with the expected and actual property values. Not every resource type supports drift detection; check the list. Decide each case: change the template to accept the new value, or update the stack to restore the old one. Import To bring an existing resource under management, add it to the template with a DeletionPolicy and run a resource import change set with the resource&#39;s identifier. Afterwards the stack manages it. Stuck states Status Meaning Action ROLLBACK_COMPLETE A create failed and rolled back Delete the stack and deploy again UPDATE_ROLLBACK_FAILED Rollback itself failed Fix the cause, then continue update rollback DELETE_FAILED A resource could not be deleted (non-empty bucket) Empty or retain it, then delete Guardrails Restrict console write access with IAM, use stack policies for critical resources, enable CloudTrail to find who changed a resource and AWS Config rules to alert on forbidden changes. Create a pipeline as the only path to production. Keeping code and cloud in agreement Flow in order: 1, Template in Git to Pipeline deploys; 2, Pipeline deploys to Resources; 3, Resources to Drift detection; 4, Drift detection to CloudTrail. Step Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Template in Git Step What it does: The intended state. If it fails or is weak: Out-of-date code misleads the next engineer. Pipeline deploys Step What it does: Only path that changes production. If it fails or is weak: Console access bypasses it. Resources Data store What it does: What actually exists. If it fails or is weak: Manual edits cause drift. Drift detection Observability What it does: Compares resources to the template on a schedule. If it fails or is weak: Unscheduled checks find drift too late. CloudTrail Observability What it does: Records who changed what. If it fails or is weak: Without it, drift has no owner. Incident: The open security group Incident A debugging change in the console left a rule open to the internet for months. $ aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource-drifts --stack-name net SecurityGroup MODIFIED IpPermissions[0].CidrIp expected 10.0.0.0/16 actual 0.0.0.0/0 Drift detection found it. The team re-applied the stack, scheduled weekly drift checks and removed console write access outside an emergency role. Common mistakes Fixing problems in the console and not in the template Never running drift detection Deleting a stack that still holds data without a retention policy Skipping resources in a failed rollback"},"cfn-bicep/bicep-syntax-resources-and-parameters":{"html":"\u003cp>Bicep removes the noise of ARM JSON. A file has parameters in, resources in the middle and outputs out, and the compiler handles the order of creation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The parts of a Bicep file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Decorators, types and secure values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dependencies and references\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deploying and converting\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-small-file\">A small file\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bicep\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"@description('Short environment name')\n@allowed(['dev', 'prod'])\nparam env string = 'dev'\n\n@secure()\nparam adminPassword string\n\nvar storageName = 'st${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}${env}'\n\nresource sa 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@2023-05-01' = {\n  name: storageName\n  location: resourceGroup().location\n  sku: { name: env == 'prod' ? 'Standard_GRS' : 'Standard_LRS' }\n  kind: 'StorageV2'\n  properties: { minimumTlsVersion: 'TLS1_2' }\n}\n\noutput blobEndpoint string = sa.properties.primaryEndpoints.blob\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">@description('Short environment name')\n@allowed(['dev', 'prod'])\nparam env string = 'dev'\n\n@secure()\nparam adminPassword string\n\nvar storageName = 'st${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}${env}'\n\nresource sa 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@2023-05-01' = {\n  name: storageName\n  location: resourceGroup().location\n  sku: { name: env == 'prod' ? 'Standard_GRS' : 'Standard_LRS' }\n  kind: 'StorageV2'\n  properties: { minimumTlsVersion: 'TLS1_2' }\n}\n\noutput blobEndpoint string = sa.properties.primaryEndpoints.blob\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>param\u003c/code> is an input; \u003ccode>@allowed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>@secure\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>@minLength\u003c/code> constrain it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>var\u003c/code> is a computed value.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>resource\u003c/code> has a symbolic name, a type with an API version, and properties. Referring to \u003ccode>sa.properties...\u003c/code> creates an \u003cstrong>implicit dependency\u003c/strong>, so Azure orders the work.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>uniqueString()\u003c/code> builds a stable, unique suffix for global names.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deploy\">Deploy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az group create --name rg-app-dev --location westeurope\naz deployment group create --resource-group rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep \\\n    --parameters env=dev adminPassword=$PW\naz bicep build --file main.bicep       # compile to ARM JSON\naz bicep decompile --file old.json     # convert ARM to Bicep\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az group create --name rg-app-dev --location westeurope\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az deployment group create --resource-group rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --parameters \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span>=dev adminPassword=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$PW\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az bicep build --file main.bicep       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># compile to ARM JSON\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az bicep decompile --file old.json     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># convert ARM to Bicep\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>.bicepparam\u003c/code> files to keep environment values out of the template.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"file&gt;comp;comp&gt;arm;arm&gt;rg;params&gt;arm\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Bicep to Azure\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"file\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">main.bicep\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"comp\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bicep compiler\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"arm\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Azure Resource Manager\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rg\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resource group\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"params\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Parameter file\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, main.bicep to Bicep compiler; 2, Bicep compiler to Azure Resource Manager; 3, Azure Resource Manager to Resource group; 4, Parameter file to Azure Resource Manager.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"file\">\u003cdt>main.bicep \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Parameters, variables, resources and outputs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old API versions miss new features.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"comp\">\u003cdt>Bicep compiler \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks types and produces an ARM template.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Errors appear before any deployment.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"arm\">\u003cdt>Azure Resource Manager \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Orders and creates the resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Failed resources leave a partial deployment.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rg\">\u003cdt>Resource group \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds the created resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deleting it removes everything inside.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"params\">\u003cdt>Parameter file \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Values for each environment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Secrets in plain files leak.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-name-that-was-not-unique\">Incident: The name that was not unique\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A storage account deploy failed because the name was already taken.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az deployment group create --resource-group rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az deployment group create --resource-group rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(StorageAccountAlreadyTaken) The storage account named acmestorage is already taken.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Storage account names are global. Building the name with uniqueString() made it stable inside the resource group and unique across Azure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Fixed names for globally unique resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets as plain parameters instead of @secure or Key Vault references\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Very old API versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing generated ARM JSON instead of the Bicep file\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-small-file","text":"A small file"},{"id":"deploy","text":"Deploy"},{"id":"incident-the-name-that-was-not-unique","text":"Incident: The name that was not unique"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Bicep file structure","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/file"},{"t":"Bicep resource reference","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/templates/"}],"text":"Bicep removes the noise of ARM JSON. A file has parameters in, resources in the middle and outputs out, and the compiler handles the order of creation. What you will learn The parts of a Bicep file Decorators, types and secure values Dependencies and references Deploying and converting A small file @description('Short environment name') @allowed(['dev', 'prod']) param env string = 'dev' @secure() param adminPassword string var storageName = 'st${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}${env}' resource sa 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@2023-05-01' = { name: storageName location: resourceGroup().location sku: { name: env == 'prod' ? 'Standard_GRS' : 'Standard_LRS' } kind: 'StorageV2' properties: { minimumTlsVersion: 'TLS1_2' } } output blobEndpoint string = sa.properties.primaryEndpoints.blob param is an input; @allowed , @secure , @minLength constrain it. var is a computed value. resource has a symbolic name, a type with an API version, and properties. Referring to sa.properties... creates an implicit dependency , so Azure orders the work. uniqueString() builds a stable, unique suffix for global names. Deploy $ az group create --name rg-app-dev --location westeurope $ az deployment group create --resource-group rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep \\ --parameters env =dev adminPassword= $PW $ az bicep build --file main.bicep # compile to ARM JSON $ az bicep decompile --file old.json # convert ARM to Bicep Use .bicepparam files to keep environment values out of the template. Bicep to Azure Flow in order: 1, main.bicep to Bicep compiler; 2, Bicep compiler to Azure Resource Manager; 3, Azure Resource Manager to Resource group; 4, Parameter file to Azure Resource Manager. Step Observability Data store Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. main.bicep Step What it does: Parameters, variables, resources and outputs. If it fails or is weak: Old API versions miss new features. Bicep compiler Observability What it does: Checks types and produces an ARM template. If it fails or is weak: Errors appear before any deployment. Azure Resource Manager Step What it does: Orders and creates the resources. If it fails or is weak: Failed resources leave a partial deployment. Resource group Data store What it does: Holds the created resources. If it fails or is weak: Deleting it removes everything inside. Parameter file Security What it does: Values for each environment. If it fails or is weak: Secrets in plain files leak. Incident: The name that was not unique Incident A storage account deploy failed because the name was already taken. $ az deployment group create --resource-group rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep (StorageAccountAlreadyTaken) The storage account named acmestorage is already taken. Storage account names are global. Building the name with uniqueString() made it stable inside the resource group and unique across Azure. Common mistakes Fixed names for globally unique resources Secrets as plain parameters instead of @secure or Key Vault references Very old API versions Editing generated ARM JSON instead of the Bicep file"},"cfn-bicep/bicep-modules-loops-and-conditions":{"html":"\u003cp>As a Bicep estate grows, copy and paste becomes the enemy. Modules give structure, loops remove repetition, and conditions keep one file useful for many environments.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Modules and their inputs and outputs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Loops over arrays and objects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Conditions and existing resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing modules via a registry\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"modules\">Modules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bicep\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"module net './modules/network.bicep' = {\n  name: 'network'\n  params: {\n    vnetName: 'vnet-${env}'\n    subnets: ['web', 'app', 'data']\n  }\n}\n\noutput vnetId string = net.outputs.vnetId\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">module net './modules/network.bicep' = {\n  name: 'network'\n  params: {\n    vnetName: 'vnet-${env}'\n    subnets: ['web', 'app', 'data']\n  }\n}\n\noutput vnetId string = net.outputs.vnetId\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each module is a Bicep file with \u003ccode>param\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>output\u003c/code>. The module runs as its own nested deployment, which appears under that name in the portal and in deployment history.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"loops-and-conditions\">Loops and conditions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bicep\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"resource subnet 'Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/subnets@2023-09-01' = [for (name, i) in subnets: {\n  parent: vnet\n  name: name\n  properties: { addressPrefix: '10.0.${i}.0/24' }\n}]\n\nresource lock 'Microsoft.Authorization/locks@2020-05-01' = if (env == 'prod') {\n  name: 'no-delete'\n  properties: { level: 'CanNotDelete' }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">resource subnet 'Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/subnets@2023-09-01' = [for (name, i) in subnets: {\n  parent: vnet\n  name: name\n  properties: { addressPrefix: '10.0.${i}.0/24' }\n}]\n\nresource lock 'Microsoft.Authorization/locks@2020-05-01' = if (env == 'prod') {\n  name: 'no-delete'\n  properties: { level: 'CanNotDelete' }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>existing\u003c/code> to refer to a resource created elsewhere: \u003ccode>resource kv &#39;Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults@2023-07-01&#39; existing = { name: kvName }\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registry\">Registry\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Publish tested modules to an \u003cstrong>Azure Container Registry\u003c/strong> with \u003ccode>az bicep publish\u003c/code>, and use them as \u003ccode>module m &#39;br:acme.azurecr.io/bicep/network:v1.2&#39; = { ... }\u003c/code>. Public ready-made modules are available under the \u003ccode>br/public\u003c/code> alias. Version modules and pin versions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"main&gt;net;main&gt;app;reg&gt;main;net&gt;rg;app&gt;rg\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Modules and loops\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"16.7\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"main\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">main.bicep\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Network module\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">App module\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"reg\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Module registry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resource group\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, main.bicep to Network module; 2, main.bicep to App module; 3, Module registry to main.bicep; 4, Network module to Resource group; 5, App module to Resource group.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"main\">\u003cdt>main.bicep \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Orchestrates modules with parameters.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Large mains become unreadable; keep it thin.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Network module \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates a vnet and subnets by looping.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing a loop key can recreate subnets.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>App module \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Uses outputs from the network module.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing outputs break the dependency.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"reg\">\u003cdt>Module registry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores versioned shared modules in ACR.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unpinned versions change under you.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rg\">\u003cdt>Resource group \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Result of all nested deployments.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Failures appear in the nested deployment name.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-six-copies-of-one-vnet\">Incident: Six copies of one vnet\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A subnet change had to be repeated in six files and one was missed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az network vnet subnet list --vnet-name vnet-eu -g rg-eu -o table\naz network vnet subnet list --vnet-name vnet-us -g rg-us -o table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az network vnet subnet list --vnet-name vnet-eu -g rg-eu -o table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web  app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az network vnet subnet list --vnet-name vnet-us -g rg-us -o table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web  app  data\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Copies drifted apart. The team created one network module with a subnet list, published it to a registry with a version, and every environment now uses it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Copy and paste instead of modules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unpinned registry module versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing loop items so resources are recreated\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Modules with dozens of parameters\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"modules","text":"Modules"},{"id":"loops-and-conditions","text":"Loops and conditions"},{"id":"registry","text":"Registry"},{"id":"incident-six-copies-of-one-vnet","text":"Incident: Six copies of one vnet"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Bicep modules","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/modules"},{"t":"Bicep loops","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/loops"}],"text":"As a Bicep estate grows, copy and paste becomes the enemy. Modules give structure, loops remove repetition, and conditions keep one file useful for many environments. What you will learn Modules and their inputs and outputs Loops over arrays and objects Conditions and existing resources Sharing modules via a registry Modules module net './modules/network.bicep' = { name: 'network' params: { vnetName: 'vnet-${env}' subnets: ['web', 'app', 'data'] } } output vnetId string = net.outputs.vnetId Each module is a Bicep file with param and output . The module runs as its own nested deployment, which appears under that name in the portal and in deployment history. Loops and conditions resource subnet 'Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/subnets@2023-09-01' = [for (name, i) in subnets: { parent: vnet name: name properties: { addressPrefix: '10.0.${i}.0/24' } }] resource lock 'Microsoft.Authorization/locks@2020-05-01' = if (env == 'prod') { name: 'no-delete' properties: { level: 'CanNotDelete' } } Use existing to refer to a resource created elsewhere: resource kv &#39;Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults@2023-07-01&#39; existing = { name: kvName } . Registry Publish tested modules to an Azure Container Registry with az bicep publish , and use them as module m &#39;br:acme.azurecr.io/bicep/network:v1.2&#39; = { ... } . Public ready-made modules are available under the br/public alias. Version modules and pin versions. Modules and loops Flow in order: 1, main.bicep to Network module; 2, main.bicep to App module; 3, Module registry to main.bicep; 4, Network module to Resource group; 5, App module to Resource group. Step Network edge Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. main.bicep Step What it does: Orchestrates modules with parameters. If it fails or is weak: Large mains become unreadable; keep it thin. Network module Network edge What it does: Creates a vnet and subnets by looping. If it fails or is weak: Changing a loop key can recreate subnets. App module Service What it does: Uses outputs from the network module. If it fails or is weak: Missing outputs break the dependency. Module registry Data store What it does: Stores versioned shared modules in ACR. If it fails or is weak: Unpinned versions change under you. Resource group Step What it does: Result of all nested deployments. If it fails or is weak: Failures appear in the nested deployment name. Incident: Six copies of one vnet Incident A subnet change had to be repeated in six files and one was missed. $ az network vnet subnet list --vnet-name vnet-eu -g rg-eu -o table web app $ az network vnet subnet list --vnet-name vnet-us -g rg-us -o table web app data Copies drifted apart. The team created one network module with a subnet list, published it to a registry with a version, and every environment now uses it. Common mistakes Copy and paste instead of modules Unpinned registry module versions Changing loop items so resources are recreated Modules with dozens of parameters"},"cfn-bicep/bicep-deployments-what-if-and-scopes":{"html":"\u003cp>Where a deployment lands and how it changes existing resources matter as much as the template. Scope, mode and a preview step are the three controls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Scopes from resource group to tenant\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Incremental and complete modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What-if previews\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deployment history and protection\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scopes\">Scopes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bicep\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"targetScope = 'subscription'\n\nresource rg 'Microsoft.Resources/resourceGroups@2024-03-01' = {\n  name: 'rg-app-${env}'\n  location: 'westeurope'\n}\n\nmodule app './app.bicep' = {\n  scope: rg\n  name: 'app'\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">targetScope = 'subscription'\n\nresource rg 'Microsoft.Resources/resourceGroups@2024-03-01' = {\n  name: 'rg-app-${env}'\n  location: 'westeurope'\n}\n\nmodule app './app.bicep' = {\n  scope: rg\n  name: 'app'\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A file targets a \u003cstrong>resource group\u003c/strong> (default), \u003cstrong>subscription\u003c/strong> (create groups, policies, role assignments), \u003cstrong>management group\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>tenant\u003c/strong>. Modules can switch scope as shown. Use the matching command: \u003ccode>az deployment group create\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>az deployment sub create\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"modes\">Modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Behaviour\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Incremental (default)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Adds and updates resources in the file; leaves others alone\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Complete\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Also deletes resources in the group that are not in the file\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-if\">What-if\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az deployment group what-if -g rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep --parameters env=dev\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az deployment group what-if -g rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep --parameters \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span>=dev\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  + Create   Microsoft.Network/publicIPAddresses/pip-app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  ~ Modify   Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/stapp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      ~ sku.name: &quot;Standard_LRS&quot; =&gt; &quot;Standard_GRS&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  - Delete   Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/vm-old\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read the symbols: \u003ccode>+\u003c/code> create, \u003ccode>~\u003c/code> modify, \u003ccode>-\u003c/code> delete, \u003ccode>=\u003c/code> no change. Put what-if in pull request checks and require approval of the output for production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"history-and-protection\">History and protection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Azure keeps up to 800 deployments per group and can delete the oldest automatically. Add \u003cstrong>resource locks\u003c/strong> (CanNotDelete) and \u003cstrong>Azure Policy\u003c/strong> to block risky changes regardless of the template.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pr&gt;wi;wi&gt;appr;appr&gt;dep;lock&gt;dep\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A safe Azure deployment\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pr\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pull request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wi\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">what-if\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"appr\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Approval\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dep\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Deployment\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lock\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Locks and Policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pull request to what-if; 2, what-if to Approval; 3, Approval to Deployment; 4, Locks and Policy to Deployment.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pr\">\u003cdt>Pull request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Bicep change reviewed by a person.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A skipped review sends untested code on.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wi\">\u003cdt>what-if \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lists creates, changes and deletes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring deletes is the classic mistake.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"appr\">\u003cdt>Approval \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Someone confirms the preview for production.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rubber stamping defeats the check.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dep\">\u003cdt>Deployment \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Applies in incremental mode.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Complete mode can delete unlisted resources.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lock\">\u003cdt>Locks and Policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Block deletes and unsafe settings regardless of code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing locks leave critical resources open to deletion.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-complete-mode-deleted-the-lot\">Incident: Complete mode deleted the lot\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A partial template ran in complete mode and removed every resource not in it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az deployment group create -g rg-app --template-file part.bicep --mode Complete\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az deployment group create -g rg-app --template-file part.bicep --mode Complete\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Deleted: Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/vm-db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Deleted: Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/stdata\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A what-if would have shown the deletes. The team restored from backup, set CanNotDelete locks on data resources and made what-if output a required step in the pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Complete mode with a template that is not the full truth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping what-if on production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No resource locks on data stores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting deployment history hit its limit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"scopes","text":"Scopes"},{"id":"modes","text":"Modes"},{"id":"what-if","text":"What-if"},{"id":"history-and-protection","text":"History and protection"},{"id":"incident-complete-mode-deleted-the-lot","text":"Incident: Complete mode deleted the lot"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Deployment what-if","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/templates/deploy-what-if"},{"t":"Deployment scopes","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/deploy-to-subscription"}],"text":"Where a deployment lands and how it changes existing resources matter as much as the template. Scope, mode and a preview step are the three controls. What you will learn Scopes from resource group to tenant Incremental and complete modes What-if previews Deployment history and protection Scopes targetScope = 'subscription' resource rg 'Microsoft.Resources/resourceGroups@2024-03-01' = { name: 'rg-app-${env}' location: 'westeurope' } module app './app.bicep' = { scope: rg name: 'app' } A file targets a resource group (default), subscription (create groups, policies, role assignments), management group or tenant . Modules can switch scope as shown. Use the matching command: az deployment group create or az deployment sub create . Modes Mode Behaviour Incremental (default) Adds and updates resources in the file; leaves others alone Complete Also deletes resources in the group that are not in the file What-if $ az deployment group what-if -g rg-app-dev --template-file main.bicep --parameters env =dev + Create Microsoft.Network/publicIPAddresses/pip-app ~ Modify Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/stapp ~ sku.name: \"Standard_LRS\" => \"Standard_GRS\" - Delete Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/vm-old Read the symbols: + create, ~ modify, - delete, = no change. Put what-if in pull request checks and require approval of the output for production. History and protection Azure keeps up to 800 deployments per group and can delete the oldest automatically. Add resource locks (CanNotDelete) and Azure Policy to block risky changes regardless of the template. A safe Azure deployment Flow in order: 1, Pull request to what-if; 2, what-if to Approval; 3, Approval to Deployment; 4, Locks and Policy to Deployment. Step Observability Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pull request Step What it does: Bicep change reviewed by a person. If it fails or is weak: A skipped review sends untested code on. what-if Observability What it does: Lists creates, changes and deletes. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring deletes is the classic mistake. Approval Security What it does: Someone confirms the preview for production. If it fails or is weak: Rubber stamping defeats the check. Deployment Step What it does: Applies in incremental mode. If it fails or is weak: Complete mode can delete unlisted resources. Locks and Policy Security What it does: Block deletes and unsafe settings regardless of code. If it fails or is weak: Missing locks leave critical resources open to deletion. Incident: Complete mode deleted the lot Incident A partial template ran in complete mode and removed every resource not in it. $ az deployment group create -g rg-app --template-file part.bicep --mode Complete Deleted: Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/vm-db Deleted: Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/stdata A what-if would have shown the deletes. The team restored from backup, set CanNotDelete locks on data resources and made what-if output a required step in the pipeline. Common mistakes Complete mode with a template that is not the full truth Skipping what-if on production No resource locks on data stores Letting deployment history hit its limit"},"cfn-bicep/deployment-stacks-and-delivery-pipelines":{"html":"\u003cp>A template is only as safe as its path to production. Deployment stacks fill a gap in Azure by tracking what a deployment owns, and a good pipeline replaces human keys with short-lived identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deployment stacks and deny settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Federated identity for pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Service roles and least privilege\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Environment promotion and approvals\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deployment-stacks\">Deployment stacks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A plain Azure deployment forgets what it created. A \u003cstrong>deployment stack\u003c/strong> keeps the list, so when a resource is removed from the template you choose whether to \u003cstrong>detach\u003c/strong> it or \u003cstrong>delete\u003c/strong> it. \u003cstrong>Deny settings\u003c/strong> can block changes to managed resources by anyone except the stack itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az stack group create --name app --resource-group rg-app --template-file main.bicep \\\n    --action-on-unmanage deleteResources --deny-settings-mode denyDelete\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az stack group create --name app --resource-group rg-app --template-file main.bicep \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --action-on-unmanage deleteResources --deny-settings-mode denyDelete\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pipelines-without-stored-keys\">Pipelines without stored keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>OpenID Connect federation\u003c/strong>: the pipeline presents a short-lived token from the CI provider, and the cloud exchanges it for a role limited to the repository, branch or environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# GitHub Actions\npermissions:\n  id-token: write\n  contents: read\nsteps:\n  - uses: azure/login@v2\n    with:\n      client-id: ${{ vars.CLIENT_ID }}\n      tenant-id: ${{ vars.TENANT_ID }}\n      subscription-id: ${{ vars.SUB }}\n  - run: az deployment group what-if -g rg-app --template-file main.bicep\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># GitHub Actions\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">permissions:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">id-token:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">write\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">contents:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">read\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">azure/login@v2\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">with:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">client-id:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">vars.CLIENT_ID\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tenant-id:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">vars.TENANT_ID\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">subscription-id:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">vars.SUB\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">az\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployment\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">group\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">what-if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-g\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rg-app\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--template-file\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main.bicep\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For AWS, the pipeline assumes a role through an OIDC provider. For CloudFormation, pass a \u003cstrong>service role\u003c/strong> so the stack uses its own permissions rather than the caller&#39;s.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"promotion\">Promotion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Build once, version the template, deploy to dev, run checks, then promote to production behind a manual approval that displays the change set or what-if output. Keep separate identities and subscriptions or accounts per environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"git&gt;ci;ci&gt;oidc;oidc&gt;dev;oidc&gt;prod\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A least-privilege pipeline\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"git\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Git commit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"oidc\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Federated identity\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dev environment\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Production\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Git commit to Pipeline; 2, Pipeline to Federated identity; 3, Federated identity to Dev environment; 4, Federated identity to Production.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"git\">\u003cdt>Git commit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Templates and parameters reviewed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unreviewed merges deploy straight to production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>Pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lints, previews and deploys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A stored key in settings can leak.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"oidc\">\u003cdt>Federated identity \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Short-lived token exchanged for a narrow role.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A too-broad role undoes the benefit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Dev environment \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> First target of every change.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping dev finds problems in production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Production \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Deployed after approval of the preview.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No approval gate means no last check.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-key-in-the-log\">Incident: A key in the log\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A long-lived cloud key stored in pipeline settings was printed by a debugging step.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">build.log: echo $AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">build.log: AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=Q8x...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">security: secret rotated; sign-ins reviewed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key was valid for years and gave wide access. Federated credentials replaced it: nothing is stored, the token lives for minutes and only the main branch&#39;s production job can assume the production role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long-lived keys in CI settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One pipeline identity with owner rights everywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No approval step for production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deployment stack deny settings that block the pipeline itself\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"deployment-stacks","text":"Deployment stacks"},{"id":"pipelines-without-stored-keys","text":"Pipelines without stored keys"},{"id":"promotion","text":"Promotion"},{"id":"incident-a-key-in-the-log","text":"Incident: A key in the log"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Deployment stacks","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/deployment-stacks"},{"t":"CloudFormation service roles","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/using-iam-servicerole.html"}],"text":"A template is only as safe as its path to production. Deployment stacks fill a gap in Azure by tracking what a deployment owns, and a good pipeline replaces human keys with short-lived identity. What you will learn Deployment stacks and deny settings Federated identity for pipelines Service roles and least privilege Environment promotion and approvals Deployment stacks A plain Azure deployment forgets what it created. A deployment stack keeps the list, so when a resource is removed from the template you choose whether to detach it or delete it. Deny settings can block changes to managed resources by anyone except the stack itself. $ az stack group create --name app --resource-group rg-app --template-file main.bicep \\ --action-on-unmanage deleteResources --deny-settings-mode denyDelete Pipelines without stored keys Use OpenID Connect federation : the pipeline presents a short-lived token from the CI provider, and the cloud exchanges it for a role limited to the repository, branch or environment. # GitHub Actions permissions: id-token: write contents: read steps: - uses: azure/login@v2 with: client-id: ${{ vars.CLIENT_ID }} tenant-id: ${{ vars.TENANT_ID }} subscription-id: ${{ vars.SUB }} - run: az deployment group what-if -g rg-app --template-file main.bicep For AWS, the pipeline assumes a role through an OIDC provider. For CloudFormation, pass a service role so the stack uses its own permissions rather than the caller&#39;s. Promotion Build once, version the template, deploy to dev, run checks, then promote to production behind a manual approval that displays the change set or what-if output. Keep separate identities and subscriptions or accounts per environment. A least-privilege pipeline Flow in order: 1, Git commit to Pipeline; 2, Pipeline to Federated identity; 3, Federated identity to Dev environment; 4, Federated identity to Production. Step Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Git commit Step What it does: Templates and parameters reviewed. If it fails or is weak: Unreviewed merges deploy straight to production. Pipeline Step What it does: Lints, previews and deploys. If it fails or is weak: A stored key in settings can leak. Federated identity Security What it does: Short-lived token exchanged for a narrow role. If it fails or is weak: A too-broad role undoes the benefit. Dev environment Data store What it does: First target of every change. If it fails or is weak: Skipping dev finds problems in production. Production Data store What it does: Deployed after approval of the preview. If it fails or is weak: No approval gate means no last check. Incident: A key in the log Incident A long-lived cloud key stored in pipeline settings was printed by a debugging step. build.log: echo $AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET build.log: AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=Q8x... security: secret rotated; sign-ins reviewed The key was valid for years and gave wide access. Federated credentials replaced it: nothing is stored, the token lives for minutes and only the main branch&#39;s production job can assume the production role. Common mistakes Long-lived keys in CI settings One pipeline identity with owner rights everywhere No approval step for production Deployment stack deny settings that block the pipeline itself"},"cfn-bicep/testing-linting-and-policy-checks-for-templates":{"html":"\u003cp>A template change can open a firewall or delete a database. Automated checks catch most of the common mistakes at no cost, in seconds, before anyone deploys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Linting for syntax and best practice\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policy as code for standards\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Previews and security scanning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test environments and smoke tests\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-layers\">The layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>CloudFormation\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Bicep\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Syntax and best practice\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cfn-lint\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>az bicep build\u003c/code>, Bicep linter\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Policy rules\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cfn-guard\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>PSRule for Azure, Azure Policy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Security scan\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Checkov, Trivy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Checkov, Trivy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Preview\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Change set\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>What-if\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Real test\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Deploy to a test account\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Deploy to a test subscription\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"examples\">Examples\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cfn-lint template.yaml\ncfn-guard validate --data template.yaml --rules s3-encryption.guard\naz bicep build --file main.bicep\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cfn-lint template.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">E3012 Property Resources/Logs/Properties/Versioning should be of type Object\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cfn-guard validate --data template.yaml --rules s3-encryption.guard\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Logs/BucketEncryption: FAIL (rule: s3_bucket_encryption_enabled)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az bicep build --file main.bicep\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Warning no-hardcoded-env-urls: Environment URLs should not be hardcoded\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A guard rule is a short statement of the standard, for example &quot;every S3 bucket has encryption&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rule s3_bucket_encryption_enabled {\n  Resources.*[ Type == 'AWS::S3::Bucket' ].Properties.BucketEncryption exists\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">rule s3_bucket_encryption_enabled {\n  Resources.*[ Type == &#x27;AWS::S3::Bucket&#x27; ].Properties.BucketEncryption exists\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-deployments\">Test deployments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Linters cannot prove that a stack really deploys, that an app responds or that permissions are enough. Create a short-lived environment in a sandbox account, run smoke tests, then delete it. Use \u003cstrong>taskcat\u003c/strong> for CloudFormation across regions. Always clean up to avoid cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"wiring\">Wiring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run checks locally with pre-commit and again in CI as the required status, so a pull request cannot merge on failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pr&gt;lint;lint&gt;pol;pol&gt;prev;prev&gt;test\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Template checks in order\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pr\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pull request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lint\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lint\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Preview\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test deployment\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pull request to Lint; 2, Lint to Policy rules; 3, Policy rules to Preview; 4, Preview to Test deployment.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pr\">\u003cdt>Pull request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Starts the checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Checks that run only on request are skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lint\">\u003cdt>Lint \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Syntax and best practice.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Warnings left alone become habits.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encryption, tags, public access rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rules nobody owns go stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prev\">\u003cdt>Preview \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Change set or what-if.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Previewing against the wrong environment misleads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>Test deployment \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Short-lived environment with smoke tests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgotten test stacks cost money.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-open-to-the-world\">Incident: Open to the world\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pull request opened a security group to the internet; policy checks caught it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cfn-guard validate --data app.yaml --rules network.guard\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cfn-guard validate --data app.yaml --rules network.guard\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sg-web: FAIL (rule: no_open_ssh)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ingress 0.0.0.0/0 on port 22\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The check failed before merge. The author restricted the source range, the review took two minutes, and no auditor had to find it later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Linting only on a developer laptop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating warnings as noise\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test stacks never cleaned up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policy rules with no owner\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-layers","text":"The layers"},{"id":"examples","text":"Examples"},{"id":"test-deployments","text":"Test deployments"},{"id":"wiring","text":"Wiring"},{"id":"incident-open-to-the-world","text":"Incident: Open to the world"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"cfn-lint","u":"https://github.com/aws-cloudformation/cfn-lint"},{"t":"Bicep linter","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/linter"}],"text":"A template change can open a firewall or delete a database. Automated checks catch most of the common mistakes at no cost, in seconds, before anyone deploys. What you will learn Linting for syntax and best practice Policy as code for standards Previews and security scanning Test environments and smoke tests The layers Layer CloudFormation Bicep Syntax and best practice cfn-lint az bicep build , Bicep linter Policy rules cfn-guard PSRule for Azure, Azure Policy Security scan Checkov, Trivy Checkov, Trivy Preview Change set What-if Real test Deploy to a test account Deploy to a test subscription Examples $ cfn-lint template.yaml E3012 Property Resources/Logs/Properties/Versioning should be of type Object $ cfn-guard validate --data template.yaml --rules s3-encryption.guard Logs/BucketEncryption: FAIL (rule: s3_bucket_encryption_enabled) $ az bicep build --file main.bicep Warning no-hardcoded-env-urls: Environment URLs should not be hardcoded A guard rule is a short statement of the standard, for example \"every S3 bucket has encryption\". rule s3_bucket_encryption_enabled { Resources.*[ Type == &#x27;AWS::S3::Bucket&#x27; ].Properties.BucketEncryption exists } Test deployments Linters cannot prove that a stack really deploys, that an app responds or that permissions are enough. Create a short-lived environment in a sandbox account, run smoke tests, then delete it. Use taskcat for CloudFormation across regions. Always clean up to avoid cost. Wiring Run checks locally with pre-commit and again in CI as the required status, so a pull request cannot merge on failure. Template checks in order Flow in order: 1, Pull request to Lint; 2, Lint to Policy rules; 3, Policy rules to Preview; 4, Preview to Test deployment. Step Observability Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pull request Step What it does: Starts the checks. If it fails or is weak: Checks that run only on request are skipped. Lint Observability What it does: Syntax and best practice. If it fails or is weak: Warnings left alone become habits. Policy rules Security What it does: Encryption, tags, public access rules. If it fails or is weak: Rules nobody owns go stale. Preview Step What it does: Change set or what-if. If it fails or is weak: Previewing against the wrong environment misleads. Test deployment Data store What it does: Short-lived environment with smoke tests. If it fails or is weak: Forgotten test stacks cost money. Incident: Open to the world Incident A pull request opened a security group to the internet; policy checks caught it. $ cfn-guard validate --data app.yaml --rules network.guard sg-web: FAIL (rule: no_open_ssh) ingress 0.0.0.0/0 on port 22 The check failed before merge. The author restricted the source range, the review took two minutes, and no auditor had to find it later. Common mistakes Linting only on a developer laptop Treating warnings as noise Test stacks never cleaned up Policy rules with no owner"},"cfn-bicep/debugging-failed-deployments-and-moving-to-terraform":{"html":"\u003cp>Failed deployments feel mysterious because the final status is vague. The details sit one level down, in the events or operations of the individual resources. A fixed method gets you there fast.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading events and operations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common errors and fixes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cleaning up stuck or partial deployments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Migrating between native tools and Terraform\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cloudformation\">CloudFormation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name app \\\n    --query 'StackEvents[?ResourceStatus==`CREATE_FAILED`].[LogicalResourceId,ResourceStatusReason]'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name app \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;StackEvents[?ResourceStatus==`CREATE_FAILED`].[LogicalResourceId,ResourceStatusReason]&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Find the \u003cstrong>earliest\u003c/strong> failed resource. Later \u003ccode>CREATE_FAILED\u003c/code> lines for dependants usually say &quot;resource creation cancelled&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"azure\">Azure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az deployment group show -g rg-app -n main --query properties.error\naz deployment operation group list -g rg-app -n main \\\n    --query &quot;[?properties.provisioningState=='Failed'].properties.statusMessage&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az deployment group show -g rg-app -n main --query properties.error\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az deployment operation group list -g rg-app -n main \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;[?properties.provisioningState==&#x27;Failed&#x27;].properties.statusMessage&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Open the operation for the failed resource; the top-level error often says only &quot;deployment failed&quot;. Check the \u003cstrong>Activity Log\u003c/strong> for who attempted what.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-causes\">Common causes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Message contains\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Cause\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Fix\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>AlreadyExists / AlreadyTaken\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Name collision\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Use generated or unique names\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>AccessDenied / AuthorizationFailed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Missing permission\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Add the specific action to the role\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>LimitExceeded / QuotaExceeded\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Account quota\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Request an increase or change region\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>InvalidTemplate / ValidationError\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Bad value or type\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Run lint and what-if\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DependsOn / ResourceNotFound\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Order or wrong scope\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Reference the resource to create a dependency\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"migrating\">Migrating\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To move to Terraform, write matching Terraform, then \u003cstrong>import\u003c/strong> each resource into Terraform state (\u003ccode>terraform import\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>aztfexport\u003c/code> for Azure), confirm \u003ccode>terraform plan\u003c/code> shows no change, set \u003ccode>DeletionPolicy: Retain\u003c/code> in CloudFormation, then remove the stack. Moving the other way follows the same steps with \u003ccode>aws cloudformation\u003c/code> resource import. Migrate one layer at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"fail&gt;ev;ev&gt;first;first&gt;cause;ev&gt;audit\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Finding the real error\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fail\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Deployment failed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ev\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Events or operations\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"first\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">First failed resource\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cause\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cause category\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"audit\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CloudTrail or Activity Log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Deployment failed to Events or operations; 2, Events or operations to First failed resource; 3, First failed resource to Cause category; 4, Events or operations to CloudTrail or Activity Log.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fail\">\u003cdt>Deployment failed \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The final status gives no detail.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reading only this line wastes time.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ev\">\u003cdt>Events or operations \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Per-resource log of what happened.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Later failures are often consequences.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"first\">\u003cdt>First failed resource \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The earliest failure holds the true reason.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A fix to later items does not help.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cause\">\u003cdt>Cause category \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Name, permission, quota, value or order.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Misreading the category sends you the wrong way.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"audit\">\u003cdt>CloudTrail or Activity Log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows who attempted the action and the denied permission.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Disabled logs leave no evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-permission-nobody-looked-at\">Incident: The permission nobody looked at\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pipeline deployment failed with a generic message for two days.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az deployment group show -g rg-app -n main --query properties.error.message\naz deployment operation group list -g rg-app -n main\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az deployment group show -g rg-app -n main --query properties.error.message\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">At least one resource deployment operation failed.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az deployment operation group list -g rg-app -n main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet  Failed  AuthorizationFailed: no permission for Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/write\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The summary hid a simple permission gap. Adding the one missing action to the pipeline role fixed it, and the team added a runbook step: open the failed operation first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading only the last error line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing later failures before the first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving owner rights to fix a permission error\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting a stack before importing its resources elsewhere\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cloudformation","text":"CloudFormation"},{"id":"azure","text":"Azure"},{"id":"common-causes","text":"Common causes"},{"id":"migrating","text":"Migrating"},{"id":"incident-the-permission-nobody-looked-at","text":"Incident: The permission nobody looked at"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Troubleshooting CloudFormation","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSCloudFormation/latest/UserGuide/troubleshooting.html"},{"t":"Troubleshoot Azure deployments","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-resource-manager/troubleshooting/common-deployment-errors"}],"text":"Failed deployments feel mysterious because the final status is vague. The details sit one level down, in the events or operations of the individual resources. A fixed method gets you there fast. What you will learn Reading events and operations Common errors and fixes Cleaning up stuck or partial deployments Migrating between native tools and Terraform CloudFormation $ aws cloudformation describe-stack-events --stack-name app \\ --query &#x27;StackEvents[?ResourceStatus==`CREATE_FAILED`].[LogicalResourceId,ResourceStatusReason]&#x27; Find the earliest failed resource. Later CREATE_FAILED lines for dependants usually say \"resource creation cancelled\". Azure $ az deployment group show -g rg-app -n main --query properties.error $ az deployment operation group list -g rg-app -n main \\ --query \"[?properties.provisioningState==&#x27;Failed&#x27;].properties.statusMessage\" Open the operation for the failed resource; the top-level error often says only \"deployment failed\". Check the Activity Log for who attempted what. Common causes Message contains Cause Fix AlreadyExists / AlreadyTaken Name collision Use generated or unique names AccessDenied / AuthorizationFailed Missing permission Add the specific action to the role LimitExceeded / QuotaExceeded Account quota Request an increase or change region InvalidTemplate / ValidationError Bad value or type Run lint and what-if DependsOn / ResourceNotFound Order or wrong scope Reference the resource to create a dependency Migrating To move to Terraform, write matching Terraform, then import each resource into Terraform state ( terraform import , or aztfexport for Azure), confirm terraform plan shows no change, set DeletionPolicy: Retain in CloudFormation, then remove the stack. Moving the other way follows the same steps with aws cloudformation resource import. Migrate one layer at a time. Finding the real error Flow in order: 1, Deployment failed to Events or operations; 2, Events or operations to First failed resource; 3, First failed resource to Cause category; 4, Events or operations to CloudTrail or Activity Log. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Deployment failed Step What it does: The final status gives no detail. If it fails or is weak: Reading only this line wastes time. Events or operations Observability What it does: Per-resource log of what happened. If it fails or is weak: Later failures are often consequences. First failed resource Step What it does: The earliest failure holds the true reason. If it fails or is weak: A fix to later items does not help. Cause category Step What it does: Name, permission, quota, value or order. If it fails or is weak: Misreading the category sends you the wrong way. CloudTrail or Activity Log Observability What it does: Shows who attempted the action and the denied permission. If it fails or is weak: Disabled logs leave no evidence. Incident: The permission nobody looked at Incident A pipeline deployment failed with a generic message for two days. $ az deployment group show -g rg-app -n main --query properties.error.message At least one resource deployment operation failed. $ az deployment operation group list -g rg-app -n main vnet Failed AuthorizationFailed: no permission for Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/write The summary hid a simple permission gap. Adding the one missing action to the pipeline role fixed it, and the team added a runbook step: open the failed operation first. Common mistakes Reading only the last error line Fixing later failures before the first Giving owner rights to fix a permission error Deleting a stack before importing its resources elsewhere"}});